Looking at this code again, I'm realizing that resolving symlinks in ``res = resolveRequest(request, `${context}/`)`` results in an issue that can affect users. If you're working on a Next.js app and you've `npm link`'d a server dependency to your project's `node_modules` (e.g. `projects/my-next-app/node_modules/my-server-dep -> ../../my-server-dep`), then `my-server-dep` will no longer be externalized, because the resolved path doesn't match `res.match(/node_modules[/\\].*\.js$/)`.

I'm curious about the original motivation behind externalizing server-imported modules found in `node_modules`. Do we do this because modules might include code that is not safe to bundle with webpack, so must be executed on Node.js directly? Wondering if we can just bundle these instead.

---

I thought of an alternative approach.

To preserve most of the existing behaviour (and continue to support `npm link` as described above), I suppose we might want ``res = resolveRequest(request, `${context}/`)`` to actually tell us whether a symlink was followed from `node_modules/<dir>` to some other location. If we do this, then we could use the "original path" to know whether the module was found in `node_modules`, e.g.

```ts
res.originalPath.match(/node_modules[/\\].*\.js$/)
```

and use the "resolved path" to tell us whether `res` resolves to the same location as `baseRes`, e.g.

```ts
// Same as above: if the package, when required from the root,
// would be different from what the real resolution would use, we
// cannot externalize it.
if (baseRes.resolvedPath !== res.resolvedPath) {
  return callback()
}
```

