not really straighforward to do using string replacements. note that these changes [are automated](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/pull/68952/files#diff-9d85a2d25346a67c95d98aa8d81b4d677b90708bdc40e03488ce0586edaade7e), we just happen to keep the output committed in here. and if anything, it'd make more sense to wrap `setImmedate` to match `setTimeout`'s signature (see below)

> The second arg of setTimeout is the timeout but ...args in `setImmediate`

this is currently safe to ignore, see comment here
https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/4cab9a260e059bc930dd5338e54d10163041bfbd/packages/next/taskfile.js#L1655-L1664
it is however a symptom of a deeper problem with this approach, and why it's a pretty nasty hack

this whole patch relies on the assumption that a `setTimeout(() => ..., 0)` here means "ASAP (but after microtasks)" and thus is valid to replace with a `setImmediate`. this may not always be true in the future -- e.g. if react intended to actually just wait until the next task, `setImmediate` would be incorrect

we could try to be somewhat defensive against possible future `setTimeout(cb, NONZERO, ...args)` calls, by doing

```js
const setTimeoutOrImmediate =
  typeof setImmediate === "function"
    ? (cb, dur = 0, ...args) => {
        if (dur === 0 && args.length === 0) {
          // likely a scheduleWork call
          return setImmediate(cb);
        }
        return setTimeout(cb, dur, ...args);
      }
    : setTimeout;
```
or perhaps throwing an error if anything is nonzero. which maybe is worth doing? but again, it's incomplete, because it'd miss legit uses of `setTimeout(..., 0)` that are intended to run in the next task, and not just ~soon

_(really the proper fix is to just use the Node build of `react-dom/server` which already does this correctly, but that's unfortunately a pretty involved change, which is why we're considering doing this hack instead)_

CC @gnoff -- what do you think we should do here? how defensive should we be? are we just hoping that, if any upstream changes would introduce issues here, our E2E tests would catch it during the sync? i could also try to hack up a way to allow-list the `setTimeout` calls we're replacing and error if a new one appears (would need e.g. babel but prob doable)

