We can't buffer the full output. For large repos i.e. many files and thus large stdout, we can easily exceed the maximum string size of javascript. I think it'd be sufficient if we keep the last two lines complete that were streamed in memory and then check that.

You could stream the output to stdout and also attach a listener `stdout.on('data', chunk => ...)` that only ever keeps a finite string in memory.

I think if we just limit it to 2 complete lines (and otherwise discard earlier stuff), it'd work and not cause string size issues.

So something like

```js
let recentStdout = "";
execaChildProcess.stdout.on("data", (chunk) => {
  recentStdout += chunk.toString("utf-8");
  // Ensure only 3 lines (1 incomplete + 2 complete) are kept in memory
  let cutoff = recentStdout.length;
  // "a \n b \n c \n d \n" -> " \n d \n"
  // "a \n b \n c \n d" -> " \n c \n d"
  // "\n b \n c \n d" -> " \n c \n d"
  // " full line \n d" -> " full line \n d"
  // "a" -> "a"
  for (let i = 0; i < 2; i += 1) {
    cutoff = recentStdout.lastIndexOf("\n", cutoff) - 1;
  }
  if (cutoff > 0 && cutoff < recentStdout.length) {
    recentStdout = recentStdout.slice(cutoff);
  }
});

```

